# saphira-image-creator target matrix.
#
# ONE row per supported boot target. Assembly is COMMON; only the partition
# plan, the bootloader invocation and the init preparation differ. That is
# why this is data rather than four scripts: a target is a row, not an
# implementation.
#
#   <name> <boot: bios|uefi> <init: openrc|systemd> <profile>
#
# Adding a target is a line here, not a new tool.
#
# The profile names INTENTIONS only. Dependency closure belongs to APK
# metadata (depends), never to an image definition, exactly as
# saphira-profiles(5) already requires of saphira-install-tools profiles.
# This file never re-enumerates a resolved closure.
bios-openrc     bios  openrc   minimal-openrc
bios-systemd    bios  systemd  minimal-systemd
bios-saphira    bios  openrc   saphira-openrc
bios-saphirad   bios  systemd  saphirad-systemd
uefi-openrc     uefi  openrc   minimal-openrc
uefi-systemd    uefi  systemd  minimal-systemd
uefi-saphira    uefi  openrc   saphira-openrc
uefi-saphirad   uefi  systemd  saphirad-systemd
# USB installer: one hybrid medium (bios-boot + ESP + installer root)
# booting both firmwares into the ram-mode installer environment.
# systemd-only by v0 decision; the installed machine's init is chosen
# in the menu, never here.
usb-installer   hybrid  systemd  usb-installer
# Dedicated installer appliance: one hybrid medium (bios-boot + ESP +
# installer root) booting both firmwares into the wizard. systemd; the
# installed system chooses its own init in the wizard.
usb-installer-appliance hybrid systemd image-installer-appliance
# USB creator appliance: BIOS-booting Saphira whose sole purpose is
# creating working images (any flavor, ext4 or ZFS) onto real disks.
# openrc by v0 decision; the flavors it writes choose their own init.
usb-creator     bios    openrc   image-creator-appliance
